e other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically","Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a estimate problem with numbers such as 73 and 4, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model numbers 73 and 4 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Estimating with the wrong place (e.g","Use the same estimate method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter"],"question":"Solve a fresh estimate problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Estimating products by rounding factors checks whether an exact answer is reasonable before or after calculating","About 70 × 4 = 280; 292 is reasonable/close","Label units when comparing 73 with 4","Estimating with the wrong place (e.g"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for estimate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for estimate, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses estimate with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for estimate; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about estimate, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with estimate, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Estimating with the wrong place (e.g.. Correction uses About 70 × 4 = 280; 292 is reasonable/close.. Why: restores accurate estimate.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w10-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise estimate.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use estimate with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify product and explain why it matters.","I can practise round independently on a new prompt.","I can notice reasonableness needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Estimating products by rounding factors checks whether an exact answer is reasonable before or after calculating. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Estimating products by rounding factors checks whether an exact answer is reasonable before or after calculating"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about estimate with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEstimate 48 × 6 ≈ 50 × 6 = 300. Exact 288 is close. An exact answer of 88 would be unreasonable.\n\nTRY FIRST\nEstimate 73 × 4 and say whether 292 is reasonable.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAbout 70 × 4 = 280; 292 is reasonable/close.","explain":"Estimating products by rounding factors checks whether an exact answer is reasonable before or after calculating.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Estimating with the wrong place (e.g. rounding 48 to 40 when a nearer estimate helps) can still work if you judge closeness carefully. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Estimate products for reasonableness.","caption":"Flag far exact answers.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Estimate</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">50×6=300</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Exact</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">288 close</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. 